5b. B1, THE COMBINATION RULE (SESSION, from what the rating is)
   The 2.8 A is a thermal rating (MAKER p.1: 4000 h at 125 C with the rated ripple applied). Two harmonics at f1 and f2 heat as
   i^2: their cross term beats at |f1 - f2|, and the can's temperature follows a beat slower than its thermal time constant and
   averages a faster one. The sheet prints no time constant; 1 s is taken as its shortest (ASSUMPTION), so a beat under
   1 / (2 pi tau) = 0.16 Hz heats as coherent at the worst relative phase, and a faster one in mean square. Both converters'
   frequencies are continuous bands and the two oscillators are independent, so EXACT coincidence p fsw = q fch is permitted:
   the worst case sits on it, whatever the time constant. The rule: on every permitted ratio p / q with p <= 20, fsw along the
   line every 0.5 kHz inside both bands, every VIN and VBAT, the coincident pairs (p m, q m) add with one common time shift
   maximised, the rest in mean square (section 5's drawn bank); section 6's bound takes every order, p > 20 by Cauchy-Schwarz
   the check's counterexample (eight cans, 396 uF, the low-ESR can 6 mOhm and 1.5 nH, its siblings 12.5 mOhm and 2.0 nH, ceramics
     4 uF, 2 mOhm, 1.5 nH; VIN 9 V, VBAT 10 V, 7.2621 A): 2.7387 A in mean square at 198.8 / 816 kHz (the check: 2.738774); 2.8547 A at
     204 / 816 kHz, the front end's 4th harmonic on the charger's fundamental (the check: 2.854635); 2.8138 A at 204 / 408 kHz
     (the check: 2.813686). The rule puts both inside: exact coincidences, coherent at the worst phase; that bank fails

6. THE BOUND (the recheck's R1/R2): EVERY CAN OVER ARBITRARY INDEPENDENT SIBLINGS, NO SAMPLING DECIDES IT
   The regions, every branch independent of every other (each harmonic may take its own worst set: only an overstatement):
   - each can: R 44.38 to 346.12 mOhm (its ballast at either end of 1.38 %, its ESR 0 to size G's 300 mOhm, the layout's 0.5 mOhm),
     L 2.0 to 5.5 nH (the can's INFERRED band, the ballast's, the layout's 0.5 nH), C 184.8 to 514.8 uF (MAKER, +-20 % and +-30 %)
   - each ceramic, 18 on VBUS20 and 3 on FE_OUT: C 4.0 to 7.0 uF, ESR 2 to 5 mOhm, ESL 0.80 to 1.50 nH (RECORD, INFERRED)
   - C190 8.0 to 11.0 nF, 20 to 80 mOhm; C191 0.80 to 1.10 nF, 50 to 200 mOhm; both 0.5 to 1.0 nH (ASSUMPTION: no maker figure
     held for C57112 or C1588; the predecessor's ESR the least damping end)
   - L11 1 to 5 nH and L16 5 to 20 nH (RECORD, INFERRED); R11 and R16 at +-1.38 % (the HoJLR2512's 1 % and 50 ppm/K over 75 K)
   The method, per bin of frequency (927 bins from 170 kHz to 3.2 GHz, 0.5 % wide to 3 MHz, 1 % to 30 MHz, 4 % above):
   - a branch's impedance R + jX lies in a rectangle (X = wL - 1/(wC) rises with w, L and C, each over its interval, the bin's w
     taken branch by branch); its admittance in the image under 1/z, bounded by arcs of circles through the origin, enclosed by
     the hull of arc samples grown by their largest sagitta (sagitta at most 1e-04 of the image's largest modulus)
   - sums of independent branches in the Minkowski sum of their enclosures (n alike: n times one); the sources' links in
     rectangles; a source side's shunt Y_s = 1/(Z_link + 1/Y_shunt) in the hull of the inverted sum
   - the can's current per ampere of a source: k Y / (Y + Y_rest); |k| = 1 / |1 + Z_link Y_shunt| bounded by a branch and bound
     over the link's rectangle (exact distance to the shunt's polygon, the sub-rectangle's radius by the triangle inequality);
     |Y| / |Y + Y_rest| bounded by a branch and bound over the can's rectangle (each sub-rectangle's disk inverted exactly)
   - the source sides' resonances are not decoupled across harmonics: L11 in 21 cells, (L16, C190) in 105 cells, each cell's
     bound per bin, the worst cell taken (the charger side's factor exceeds 1.05 from 2.2 to 176.7 MHz: the L16 and C190 series
     resonance and C191's above it)
   - the sources: closed-form Fourier coefficients to the 120th harmonic; fSW in 0.5 kHz intervals over B3's envelope and fCH in 2 kHz
     intervals over both rows, each harmonic at the larger of its ends (convex in the ripple, which is 1/f) and at the largest bin
     bound over its frequency interval; VIN 9 to 36 V on 26 points (every 0.5 V of the boost range) and VBAT every 0.05 V (the
     operating grid: sampled, not bounded); exact coincidences fch / fsw = p / q, p <= 20, added coherently (|a| + |c| at every
     coincident frequency, any phase), every higher order by Cauchy-Schwarz; above the 120th harmonic |c_m| <= J/(2 pi m) +
     K/(2 pi m)^2 (J the waveform's jumps, K its slope jumps) at the bins' bound, and |T| <= 1 above 3.2 GHz (every branch inductive
     there, so no sum of them is smaller than one of them)
   The decision (SESSION): catalogue values ascending from 38 mOhm, the binding 7 mOhm outcome first; the first whose bound meets
   the rule at both outcomes is taken. Below 38 mOhm one feasible configuration (feasible() in the source) is over the limit at
   7 mOhm, so no bound can meet it:
